<em>Bloomberg</em>'s Mark Gurman today said the new MacBook Pro models with OLED displays will be "significantly lighter" than the existing MacBook Pro models.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/10/M5-MacBook-Pro.jpg" alt="" width="1960" height="1102" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1023242" /><br/>
"Apple has reengineered the machine's interior, miniaturizing parts and rearranging components to make the computer much easier to carry," he said, in his <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-10-04/apple-s-john-ternus-leads-design-touch-screen-oled-macbook-pro-gets-lighter"><em>Power On</em> newsletter</a>. Currently, the MacBook Pro weighs up to 4.7 pounds, while the MacBook Air weighs up to 3.3 pounds and the MacBook Neo weighs 2.7 pounds. <br/>
<br/>
With this newly-reported detail, the new models are now rumored to have six new features:<ul><li>OLED display<li></li><br/>
<li>Touch-screen capabilities</li><br/>
<li>Dynamic Island instead of a notch</li><br/>
<li>M5 Pro and M5 Max chips</li><br/>
<li>Thinner design</li><br/>
<li>"Significantly lighter" design</li></ul>Thinner and lighter go hand in hand.<br/>
<br/>
Apple plans to release the MacBook Pro models with OLED displays <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/01/oled-macbook-pro-release-date/">in October or November</a> this year, according to Gurman. In his newsletter, he reiterated that Apple is testing the laptops with macOS 27.2, which is <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/21/apple-seeds-macos-27-2-beta-2/">currently in beta</a>.<br/>
